1
00:00:02,393 --> 00:00:03,560
INSTRUCTOR: Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:03,560 --> 00:00:07,920
And welcome to the content and
video for tree data structure,

3
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:08,420
OK.

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:11,480
So now that we've covered the
list-like data structures,

5
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:15,230
like lists, arrays,
queues and stacks,

6
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:16,940
we're going to talk about trees.

7
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:21,410
So tree data structures
provide some optimization

8
00:00:21,410 --> 00:00:25,220
with a lot of the operations
you do in a data structure,

9
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:28,280
putting data inside there,
finding it, deleting it.

10
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:31,760
And those operations can be
done a lot faster in a tree

11
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:34,220
than they can in a
list data structure.

12
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:40,190
And to demonstrate that
or illustrate that,

13
00:00:40,190 --> 00:00:44,070
let's bring back up the
example of a phone book.

14
00:00:44,070 --> 00:00:46,880
So in a phone book, since
everything's sorted,

15
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:50,630
recall that you can find
something in log n time or log

16
00:00:50,630 --> 00:00:54,650
base 2n because 2 being every
time you open up the phone book

17
00:00:54,650 --> 00:00:58,640
in half, you've removed half of
the phone book that needs to be

18
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:00,030
searched again.

19
00:01:00,030 --> 00:01:02,030
So if you just keep
searching in halves,

20
00:01:02,030 --> 00:01:04,890
half, half, half, half,
half, the number of page

21
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:07,560
turns it takes you to find
something in a phone book

22
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:09,870
is log base 2 of n.

23
00:01:09,870 --> 00:01:12,270
So the idea with
the tree structure

24
00:01:12,270 --> 00:01:17,400
is, let's take advantage of that
log n ability in a find, insert,

25
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,340
and delete opposed to the log--

26
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,950
excuse me, opposed
to order n of a

27
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:25,750
find, insert, and
delete in a list.

28
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:30,030
So that's what we're after
is the speed of log n, OK.

29
00:01:30,030 --> 00:01:33,868
So let's take a look at
some tree structures here.

30
00:01:33,868 --> 00:01:35,910
First, the bulk of what
we're going to talk about

31
00:01:35,910 --> 00:01:38,760
is a binary search tree, a BST.

32
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:42,480
But to set that up, let's
have just a quick look

33
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:44,080
at a binary tree.

34
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,650
So a binary tree, as
you can see right here,

35
00:01:47,650 --> 00:01:51,280
a binary tree has nodes
and then children.

36
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:56,170
They're also referred
to as inner nodes,

37
00:01:56,170 --> 00:01:58,500
if they're inside the
tree or leaf nodes

38
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:01,620
if they're at the edge of a
tree and have no children,

39
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:02,707
that's a leaf node.

40
00:02:02,707 --> 00:02:04,290
And then there's the
root node, if you

41
00:02:04,290 --> 00:02:06,090
can see where my mouse is
hovering right now at the top

42
00:02:06,090 --> 00:02:06,600
here.

43
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:09,669
Number one is the root node
or the beginning of the tree.

44
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:13,410
All these inside nodes or inner
nodes and then these leaf nodes

45
00:02:13,410 --> 00:02:15,780
are at the end here, OK.

46
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:18,060
So this is a binary tree.

47
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:21,970
But so what the heck are nodes
or what do they represent?

48
00:02:21,970 --> 00:02:27,250
They're the exact same thing as
whatever you stored in the list.

49
00:02:27,250 --> 00:02:30,690
So if you have a list of
floats, the tree version of that

50
00:02:30,690 --> 00:02:33,550
would just be a node
containing a float.

51
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:39,140
So just like we don't really
call spaces in a list nodes,

52
00:02:39,140 --> 00:02:40,640
but that's really
just all they are.

53
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:42,810
So a space inside a list.

54
00:02:42,810 --> 00:02:47,790
For example, you start counting
at index 0 in a list, here,

55
00:02:47,790 --> 00:02:49,990
you would start at
the root of the tree.

56
00:02:49,990 --> 00:02:52,860
But that's really all they are,
just a space to store data.

57
00:02:52,860 --> 00:02:55,470
And as you can see, though,
they have a different structure.

58
00:02:55,470 --> 00:02:59,440
Instead of one node next
to another, like a list,

59
00:02:59,440 --> 00:03:01,890
here, there's a child
relationship, a parent

60
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:03,340
and child relationship.

61
00:03:03,340 --> 00:03:06,130
So the root is the parent
of all the other nodes.

62
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:08,260
And then you can see,
it's got two children.

63
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:10,690
Hence, the name binary,
refers to two children.

64
00:03:10,690 --> 00:03:14,940
So the left child is node
two and the right child

65
00:03:14,940 --> 00:03:17,100
is node three, OK.

66
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:18,987
So that's the rule
of a binary tree.

67
00:03:18,987 --> 00:03:20,070
And again, why do we care?

68
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:22,890
It's because we want to
get to where we have find,

69
00:03:22,890 --> 00:03:26,040
insert, and delete
operations in log n time

70
00:03:26,040 --> 00:03:29,720
much faster than order n time.

71
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:31,970
All right, so now, let's add
one little piece to this.

72
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:35,880
So binary tree, in
practice really, I've

73
00:03:35,880 --> 00:03:38,050
never used a binary
tree in my life.

74
00:03:38,050 --> 00:03:42,090
I've used binary search trees,
a specific type of binary tree

75
00:03:42,090 --> 00:03:43,500
often.

76
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:46,320
So here's the one rule we add
to a binary tree to make it

77
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:47,350
a binary search tree.

78
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:48,270
Here's the rule.

79
00:03:48,270 --> 00:03:53,820
That every child on the left
has to be smaller than the node.

80
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:57,070
And every child on the right
has to be greater than the node.

81
00:03:57,070 --> 00:03:59,000
So let's start with
the root node here, 8.

82
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,650
So if the root node is
8, everything on the left

83
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:02,590
must be smaller.

84
00:04:02,590 --> 00:04:05,290
Well, is 3, 1, 6, 4, and
7 all smaller than 8?

85
00:04:05,290 --> 00:04:05,970
Yes.

86
00:04:05,970 --> 00:04:08,890
On the right side, is 10, 14,
and 13 all smaller than 8?

87
00:04:08,890 --> 00:04:09,970
Yes, they all are.

88
00:04:09,970 --> 00:04:12,870
So this binary
search tree follows

89
00:04:12,870 --> 00:04:14,950
that rule of everything
on the left smaller.

90
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:17,019
And you can see all of the
inner nodes follow that as well.

91
00:04:17,019 --> 00:04:17,769
Look at this 3.

92
00:04:17,769 --> 00:04:20,740
Everything on the left
of the 3, 1 is smaller.

93
00:04:20,740 --> 00:04:24,400
Everything on the right of the
3, 6, 4, 7 is greater than.

94
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:27,760
And that's true for all
of the nodes in the tree.

95
00:04:27,760 --> 00:04:29,680
So that's a binary search tree.

96
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:32,320
And a binary search
tree, that's where

97
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:37,420
you start to get the phone
book ability, the ability to

98
00:04:37,420 --> 00:04:38,930
divide the data in half.

99
00:04:38,930 --> 00:04:40,720
So when I look for
something, say, I'm

100
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:43,900
looking for the number 6 here,
well, if I start at the root 8,

101
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:46,270
I know 6 is less than
8, go to the left.

102
00:04:46,270 --> 00:04:50,190
Then I get to the node
with a value 3, OK,

103
00:04:50,190 --> 00:04:52,140
6 is greater than
3, go to the right.

104
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:53,500
And then I go to the right here.

105
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:55,960
So it's like that phone.

106
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:57,060
Like I divide--

107
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:58,950
I'm dividing my tree
in half each time,

108
00:04:58,950 --> 00:05:00,928
looking for what
I want because I

109
00:05:00,928 --> 00:05:02,970
know I don't need to search
that half of the tree

110
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:05,380
at all, if it's not
what I'm looking for.

111
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:07,560
So that's what
gives us that order

112
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:11,920
log n speed of a binary
search tree, all right.

113
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:20,564
So let's do some
examples here, OK.

114
00:05:20,564 --> 00:05:27,170
So I'm going to take these
nodes here and do some--

115
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:28,500
insert them into a tree.

116
00:05:28,500 --> 00:05:29,480
So I'll start with 3.

117
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:31,850
These are the nodes here,
3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6.

118
00:05:31,850 --> 00:05:33,690
And I'm going to insert
them into a tree.

119
00:05:33,690 --> 00:05:36,040
So first, we have the root 3.

120
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:38,500
1, we go to the left.

121
00:05:38,500 --> 00:05:39,130
Add 1 there.

122
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:42,400
4, we insert, go
to the right there.

123
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:43,390
1 is a duplicate.

124
00:05:43,390 --> 00:05:45,340
There's a couple of ways
to handle duplicates.

125
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:48,902
In practice, you can either
have a counter on each node

126
00:05:48,902 --> 00:05:50,360
and then just
increment the counter

127
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:51,943
and say, hey, there's
a duplicate one,

128
00:05:51,943 --> 00:05:53,030
just add a counter.

129
00:05:53,030 --> 00:05:56,680
So count would be 1 here already
and you make it counter 2.

130
00:05:56,680 --> 00:05:58,910
Or in practice, you
can just add it.

131
00:05:58,910 --> 00:06:01,550
If you make a rule, just
add duplicates to the left.

132
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:02,280
That's fine too.

133
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:03,530
And I'm going to do that here.

134
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:05,150
So 1 gets added there.

135
00:06:05,150 --> 00:06:08,200
5, we start at the root,
we go to the right.

136
00:06:08,200 --> 00:06:10,780
5 is greater than 3,
5 is greater than 4.

137
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:14,060
As soon as we hit an
empty, we insert it.

138
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:16,670
In fact, maybe I
should point out here,

139
00:06:16,670 --> 00:06:21,743
if we have a leaf node here,
it still has two children,

140
00:06:21,743 --> 00:06:22,910
it's just that they're null.

141
00:06:22,910 --> 00:06:24,170
They're empty values.

142
00:06:24,170 --> 00:06:26,990
And that'll come into play when
we write the code for this.

143
00:06:26,990 --> 00:06:29,780
When we hit a null value,
that's when we know,

144
00:06:29,780 --> 00:06:33,260
OK, this is where I'm going
to insert my node, all right.

145
00:06:33,260 --> 00:06:34,650
So next up, we have a 9.

146
00:06:34,650 --> 00:06:36,860
We start from the root,
9 is greater than 3,

147
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:39,140
9 is greater than 4,
9 is greater than 5.

148
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:42,240
And we insert a 9.

149
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:43,350
2 is less than 3.

150
00:06:43,350 --> 00:06:45,330
2 is greater than 1.

151
00:06:45,330 --> 00:06:47,430
2 is inserted right there.

152
00:06:47,430 --> 00:06:50,980
6 is greater than 3, greater
than 4, greater than 5,

153
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:51,710
less than 9.

154
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:58,275
And here's our binary
search tree, OK.

155
00:06:58,275 --> 00:07:01,650
So then when we
want to do a find,

156
00:07:01,650 --> 00:07:09,600
let's say we do a find of
5, a find of 5 here, OK.

157
00:07:09,600 --> 00:07:11,490
We start at the root
5 is greater than 3

158
00:07:11,490 --> 00:07:13,670
and greater than 4,
and then we hit the 5.

159
00:07:13,670 --> 00:07:16,870
So as soon as we find
this, we return true.

160
00:07:16,870 --> 00:07:18,450
We return true.

161
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:21,340
And that return true goes
back to the 4, back to the 3,

162
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:27,940
and then is returned to the find
5 function call as true, OK.

163
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:29,230
So when is it not true?

164
00:07:29,230 --> 00:07:32,650
What will be the case
when we cannot find the 5?

165
00:07:32,650 --> 00:07:35,330
Well, it's actually as
simple as when we hit a null.

166
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:38,375
So for example, what if
we did a find 7 here.

167
00:07:41,520 --> 00:07:43,020
7 does not exist in the tree.

168
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:46,080
We start at root, go to the
right, 7 is greater than 4,

169
00:07:46,080 --> 00:07:50,230
7 is greater than 5, 7 is less
than 9, 7 is greater than 6,

170
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:52,300
and we hit a null.

171
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:53,870
I'll even add 1.

172
00:07:53,870 --> 00:07:54,850
There we go.

173
00:07:54,850 --> 00:07:56,690
Then I'll add 2.

174
00:07:56,690 --> 00:07:57,610
We hit a null.

175
00:07:57,610 --> 00:08:00,120
Well, null then,
we return false.

176
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:04,250
So this return false is returned
back up to 6, 9, 5, 4, 3.

177
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:07,130
And then find 7 is true.

178
00:08:07,130 --> 00:08:09,920
Excuse me, find 7 is false, OK.

179
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:13,360
So I need to point out
one concept that I've

180
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:16,360
been glossing over quickly.

181
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:21,620
The operations in a tree can
be performed recursively.

182
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:24,550
So recall from our
definition of recursion,

183
00:08:24,550 --> 00:08:29,020
a recursive problem
is when the logic

184
00:08:29,020 --> 00:08:31,150
to solve the superset
of the problem

185
00:08:31,150 --> 00:08:35,200
is the same as the logic to
solve the subset of the problem.

186
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,960
OK, that's a wordy
explanation, but it's actually

187
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:39,919
a really practical example here.

188
00:08:39,919 --> 00:08:42,460
So when I take this tree,
when I'm looking for 7,

189
00:08:42,460 --> 00:08:46,450
the logic on how to get it,
if the tree is this size,

190
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:50,930
is the exact same as if the
tree is only 4 and then down.

191
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:53,780
So the logic I start up
here, 3, I go to the right,

192
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:55,572
it's the exact same
logic all the way down.

193
00:08:55,572 --> 00:08:57,572
Go to the left, go to the
right, go to the left,

194
00:08:57,572 --> 00:08:59,810
go to the right. go down,
go down, go down, go down,

195
00:08:59,810 --> 00:09:01,340
hit a null 7.

196
00:09:01,340 --> 00:09:05,870
Well, in recursion, what
stops the recursion?

197
00:09:05,870 --> 00:09:07,250
The base case.

198
00:09:07,250 --> 00:09:10,210
So in my base case, I'm going
to be looking for either I

199
00:09:10,210 --> 00:09:12,680
found my number in
the case of find 5,

200
00:09:12,680 --> 00:09:15,230
my base case is going to
look for the number I found,

201
00:09:15,230 --> 00:09:18,260
or it's going to
look for a null.

202
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:20,690
So those are my base
cases and the operations

203
00:09:20,690 --> 00:09:22,850
of a binary search
tree for the find.

204
00:09:22,850 --> 00:09:25,800
Did I find what I'm looking
for or did I hit a null?

205
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:27,930
Then those base cases return.

206
00:09:27,930 --> 00:09:30,740
In the case of find 5,
it's going to hit the 5

207
00:09:30,740 --> 00:09:32,930
and return true,
return true, return

208
00:09:32,930 --> 00:09:35,850
true to the original function
call, and then you're done.

209
00:09:35,850 --> 00:09:36,890
That's recursion.

210
00:09:36,890 --> 00:09:38,252
Same thing with the find 7.

211
00:09:38,252 --> 00:09:40,460
I make a function call,
function call, function call,

212
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:41,043
function call.

213
00:09:41,043 --> 00:09:43,530
Hit a null, base case
triggers, return false,

214
00:09:43,530 --> 00:09:46,040
return false, false,
false, false, false, false.

215
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:50,030
Recursively find 7,
function call returns false.

216
00:09:50,030 --> 00:09:54,980
So trees are also a perfect
candidate for recursion.

217
00:09:54,980 --> 00:09:58,100
And you'll find that when you
do these operations recursively,

218
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:01,350
it actually makes the code
very small and concise.

219
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:05,630
Because remember, the logic
to do this on a tree of size 1

220
00:10:05,630 --> 00:10:09,870
is the same exact logic to
do it of size 1 million.

221
00:10:09,870 --> 00:10:12,290
You don't need more for
loops and while loops

222
00:10:12,290 --> 00:10:15,050
and if statements to handle
a tree of size 1 million,

223
00:10:15,050 --> 00:10:18,270
it's the same exact logic
if it's the tree of size 1.

224
00:10:18,270 --> 00:10:20,900
So once you get your recursive
function, which is just

225
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:24,050
a few lines of code for
the find and the insert,

226
00:10:24,050 --> 00:10:26,130
it works no matter
what size the tree is.

227
00:10:26,130 --> 00:10:28,190
So it makes it a perfect
candidate for recursion

228
00:10:28,190 --> 00:10:31,970
and the recursive solution
a lot more simple, OK.

229
00:10:31,970 --> 00:10:36,440
Last thing we'll cover here
on the board is delete.

230
00:10:36,440 --> 00:10:39,420
So now, let's say we're
going to delete this 5,

231
00:10:39,420 --> 00:10:43,050
there's one caveat you need
to keep in mind when you do.

232
00:10:43,050 --> 00:10:44,140
In fact, I'm going to--

233
00:10:44,140 --> 00:10:45,640
I'm going to change
on the fly here.

234
00:10:45,640 --> 00:10:48,110
Let's do a 10.

235
00:10:48,110 --> 00:10:49,313
And then we'll do--

236
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:54,100
let's do another
10 because we're

237
00:10:54,100 --> 00:10:55,550
following the rule duplicates.

238
00:10:55,550 --> 00:10:57,880
Go to the left and then 11, OK.

239
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:00,840
I'm adding this really
quick here to 2.

240
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:03,340
There's one caveat you have to
keep in mind with the delete.

241
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,210
And I'm adding a
little bit of the tree,

242
00:11:05,210 --> 00:11:06,430
so I can show you that.

243
00:11:06,430 --> 00:11:14,648
OK, so now, we're going
to delete the 9, OK.

244
00:11:14,648 --> 00:11:16,090
So we're going to delete the 9.

245
00:11:16,090 --> 00:11:18,760
Well, to delete the 9,
first we have to find it.

246
00:11:18,760 --> 00:11:22,090
So a delete is
identical to a find.

247
00:11:22,090 --> 00:11:23,700
It's just that once
you find it, you

248
00:11:23,700 --> 00:11:25,380
need to delete it
out of the tree

249
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:28,320
and adjust the tree so it's
still a binary search tree, OK.

250
00:11:28,320 --> 00:11:32,440
So first step in the delete
is find it, 3, 4, 5, 9,

251
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:33,490
we found it.

252
00:11:33,490 --> 00:11:36,900
So can we just delete
it and call it good?

253
00:11:36,900 --> 00:11:39,520
Well, no, we can't
have a binary search

254
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:41,830
tree that just has something
missing in the middle.

255
00:11:41,830 --> 00:11:43,820
It just has a non-existent node.

256
00:11:43,820 --> 00:11:47,200
We need to readjust this
tree, so that it's still

257
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:49,200
a valid binary search tree.

258
00:11:49,200 --> 00:11:50,800
So how do we do that?

259
00:11:50,800 --> 00:11:52,880
Well, what are our options here?

260
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:56,380
We could bring up the 6 here.

261
00:11:56,380 --> 00:11:58,300
And then would that
still be valid?

262
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:00,933
6 is greater than 5 and
10 is greater than 6.

263
00:12:00,933 --> 00:12:01,850
Yeah, that would work.

264
00:12:01,850 --> 00:12:05,210
But I added this to show you
one problem that can arise.

265
00:12:05,210 --> 00:12:07,700
If this tree right here,
if the next layer is full,

266
00:12:07,700 --> 00:12:09,140
can we bring this 10 up?

267
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:11,390
No, because 10 has
two children already.

268
00:12:11,390 --> 00:12:13,510
So if we bring
this 10 up, 6 would

269
00:12:13,510 --> 00:12:14,930
become the left child of 10.

270
00:12:14,930 --> 00:12:17,613
Well, 10 already has a left
child and a right child.

271
00:12:17,613 --> 00:12:19,780
And you can't have three
children in a binary search

272
00:12:19,780 --> 00:12:20,280
tree.

273
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:22,270
So what do we do to--

274
00:12:22,270 --> 00:12:23,948
so this 6 doesn't have children.

275
00:12:23,948 --> 00:12:24,740
But what if it did?

276
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:26,500
What if this 6 tree
was full right here

277
00:12:26,500 --> 00:12:28,390
and this 10 tree
was full right here?

278
00:12:28,390 --> 00:12:31,330
Well, we'd have to make
a rule of something

279
00:12:31,330 --> 00:12:33,670
to bring up to become
the new member,

280
00:12:33,670 --> 00:12:35,520
to become this new node here.

281
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:37,890
And there's two
options you can do.

282
00:12:37,890 --> 00:12:42,530
You can either find the
max in the left tree.

283
00:12:42,530 --> 00:12:44,880
So let's see, let me just
add a max really quick here.

284
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:50,090
So let's say that was a 9,
this is going to be a 7, OK.

285
00:12:50,090 --> 00:12:54,510
We can either find the max in
our left tree, so bring 7 up.

286
00:12:57,500 --> 00:12:58,980
And the structure is intact.

287
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:03,140
And the reason we can find
the max, if we have a max,

288
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:05,700
it means there's no right child.

289
00:13:05,700 --> 00:13:07,950
There's no right child,
if that's the maximum.

290
00:13:07,950 --> 00:13:09,890
So then we can
bring it up, and it

291
00:13:09,890 --> 00:13:12,290
won't hurt any--
it won't mess up

292
00:13:12,290 --> 00:13:15,740
the structure of adding any new
right children because it never

293
00:13:15,740 --> 00:13:16,970
had a right child.

294
00:13:16,970 --> 00:13:19,020
Let me restate that a
little more clearly.

295
00:13:19,020 --> 00:13:21,410
If I find the max
on my left subtree,

296
00:13:21,410 --> 00:13:24,140
it will not have a right child
because that right child would

297
00:13:24,140 --> 00:13:26,310
be greater than, and
that would be the max.

298
00:13:26,310 --> 00:13:29,310
So the max does not have a right
child, we can bring that up.

299
00:13:29,310 --> 00:13:30,740
And then any right
child that did

300
00:13:30,740 --> 00:13:34,080
exist can just become the
new right child of that node.

301
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,200
And then the structure
of the BST is maintained.

302
00:13:36,200 --> 00:13:37,440
And that works great.

303
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:39,200
The other option,
you may have guessed,

304
00:13:39,200 --> 00:13:42,830
we can either bring up the
max in the left subtree

305
00:13:42,830 --> 00:13:46,460
or the minimum in
the right subtree.

306
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:49,700
Because the same idea, the
minimum in the right subtree

307
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:51,690
will not have a left child.

308
00:13:51,690 --> 00:13:54,770
So in this case,
our minimum is 10.

309
00:13:54,770 --> 00:13:57,480
Bring up this 10, it does
not have a left subchild.

310
00:13:57,480 --> 00:14:01,850
So any left subtree that
this deleted node had,

311
00:14:01,850 --> 00:14:05,030
we can simply attach to
it as its new left subtree

312
00:14:05,030 --> 00:14:06,740
because we know it
didn't have any,

313
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:10,680
if it came up as the max
node in the right subtree.

314
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:12,890
So we bring that node up,
it becomes the new node.

315
00:14:12,890 --> 00:14:20,830
And then the structure of the
BST has remained in place, OK.

316
00:14:20,830 --> 00:14:24,180
So that is why binary
search trees are used today

317
00:14:24,180 --> 00:14:27,450
because it's a way, a simple way
to have a data structure that

318
00:14:27,450 --> 00:14:30,900
gets you find,
inserts, and deletes

319
00:14:30,900 --> 00:14:34,910
in order log n time, all right.

320
00:14:34,910 --> 00:14:37,410
To conclude this video, I'm
going to show you one more thing

321
00:14:37,410 --> 00:14:37,910
here.

322
00:14:41,570 --> 00:14:44,270
So we've talked about structure.

323
00:14:44,270 --> 00:14:50,480
We've talked about the
point of a binary search

324
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:54,140
tree is to get order log
n findings or deletes.

325
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:56,420
This might be a new
data structure to you,

326
00:14:56,420 --> 00:15:00,060
but it's actually used
pretty heavily in the world.

327
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:02,360
And now, you know what
a binary search tree is,

328
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:06,080
you might notice it more or
see where it's a better data

329
00:15:06,080 --> 00:15:07,250
structure for.

330
00:15:07,250 --> 00:15:12,200
But just as a fun
example, I put into GPT,

331
00:15:12,200 --> 00:15:14,810
what are some examples
of BST in use today?

332
00:15:14,810 --> 00:15:17,060
And it gave me binary search
trees or widely used data

333
00:15:17,060 --> 00:15:17,720
structures.

334
00:15:17,720 --> 00:15:20,630
Databases, many database systems
use BST to store and retrieve

335
00:15:20,630 --> 00:15:21,680
data efficiently.

336
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:24,390
Well, how is it stored
and retrieved efficiently?

337
00:15:24,390 --> 00:15:26,210
It's stored and
retrieved efficiently

338
00:15:26,210 --> 00:15:28,310
with order log n time.

339
00:15:28,310 --> 00:15:31,470
Log n is way faster
than order n time.

340
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:33,240
So database is a great fit.

341
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:35,570
File systems are a
great uses for trees.

342
00:15:35,570 --> 00:15:36,860
And you can see that.

343
00:15:36,860 --> 00:15:40,910
If you're using a Mac OS X
operating system or a Linux

344
00:15:40,910 --> 00:15:43,950
operating system,
the root directory

345
00:15:43,950 --> 00:15:46,180
is the root node of the tree.

346
00:15:46,180 --> 00:15:48,678
So that's a direct comparison.

347
00:15:48,678 --> 00:15:50,970
Windows is a little bit
different, Windows uses drives.

348
00:15:50,970 --> 00:15:54,960
But just think of those drives
like mini roots themselves

349
00:15:54,960 --> 00:15:58,210
or multiple tree structure.

350
00:15:58,210 --> 00:16:01,980
But all of the big three
operating systems, Mac OS X,

351
00:16:01,980 --> 00:16:08,080
Linux, and Windows all use
binary search trees, OK.

352
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:11,440
Also, symbol tables
or just network

353
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:14,210
routing and spell checkers,
there's language models.

354
00:16:14,210 --> 00:16:16,420
There's a lot of uses
for binary search trees

355
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:21,910
because they get you the
speed of entering data, OK.

356
00:16:21,910 --> 00:16:25,190
That is a overview of
binary search trees.

357
00:16:25,190 --> 00:16:27,180
Thanks for watching.